CHARACTERISATION, CHECKED rather than read off the three printed rows
   claim: for even n, N(n) = { (w,c) : w+c = n, w>=c>=1 }  UNION
                            { (a,b,1) : a+b = n-1, a>=b>=1 },  and nothing else.
   mismatches over n = 4..32 even : 0  -> CHARACTERISATION HOLDS
   NO near-miss has k >= 4 anywhere in this range: for k >= 4 the observed slack
   is s0(lambda) >= lambda_1 + 2. That is STRICTLY STRONGER than C1 asks, and it
   is the first structural statement this line has about k >= 4 at all.
   STATED AS WHAT IT IS: machine-observed to n = 32, NOT proved, and this line has
   been burned exactly once this session by treating a census as a theorem.

   READ IT OFF: the k=2 members are exactly Theorem C1-2's family -- (w,c) with
   w+c = n even, 1 <= c <= w -- which is n/2 partitions. The REMAINING n/2 - 1
   near-misses have k >= 3, and THEY are the objects a k >= 3 proof must handle.
   The count n-1 = n/2 + (n/2 - 1) is therefore a statement with a k=2 half this
   line has already PROVED and a k>=3 half it has not. That is the split to attack.

